WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.260
Hi, my name is Chris Gagne, and I'm an agile coach and today. I'm going to talk about the definition of ready

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.480
The definition of ready is a documented team agreement.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:19.320
It defines the conditions that must be met for a product backlog item to be considered ready to work on by the team

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.439
It's not a formal contract or approval gate,

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:29.760
but rather a set of guidelines that will help your team make a good trade-off between minimizing risks and getting started.

00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:33.539
Creating and following the definition of ready,

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:40.469
diligently, can easily double the speed in the agile team because the team can stay focused on shipping working software,

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.230
not chasing down requirements. For a real-world example, think of the "Mise en place" or

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.309
everything in this place concept used by chefs and cooking shows.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:57.210
Before they begin preparing the recipe, they carefully organize and measure the various ingredients

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:03.580
so they're immediately at hand while cooking. A little careful preparation goes a long way!

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:09.720
A good starting point is the I.N.V.E.S.T mnemonic, to which I like to add a "U", for "you" invest.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.880
"U" stands for "user experience", which includes research, wireframes,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.560
mocks, written content, and even visual design.  Now, mature scrum teams with dedicated designers can

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:27.560
research, design, code, tests, and even ship within a single two-week sprint.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.140
If you can't, consider making user experience a prerequisite for development work beginning.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.900
"I" means "Independent".

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:43.200
The backlog item should be self-contained in a way that there's no in-sprint dependency on another backlog item.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:45.960
"N" means "Negotiable".

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:53.560
Until the sprint formally starts, the development team and product owner can negotiate on the nature and scope of the item.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:59.640
The corollary: once the sprint starts, the product owner should _not_ materially change the acceptance criteria.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.860
"V" means "Valuable".

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:08.220
An item must deliver value to your stakeholders. If you can't clearly articulate the value for the work,

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.470
you probably shouldn't be doing it.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.000
"E" means "Estimable".

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.500
The team must be able to estimate the size of the backlog item, ideally using story points.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.000
Otherwise, you won't be able to effectively manage your flow of work.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.060
"S" means "Small".

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.840
Ideally the backlog item should not take the team more than half a sprint to complete.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.300
"T"  means "Testable".

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.080
The backlog item and acceptance criteria

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.520
should contain enough information to make test development possible,

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:42.689
for instance, "The coffee should be between 120 and

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.149
130 degrees Fahrenheit".  It's much clearer Acceptance Criteria, then "The coffee should be

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.290
hot".

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:53.910
Create your definition of ready, as a team, based on your unique needs.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.600
Review it at each backlog refinement and sprint planning meeting.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.500
You can update your definition of ready at any time, so long as your team agrees to change.

